00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.970
- Yeah, so really exciting.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.870
We've built 10 new courses in math courses

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:26.490
that are kinda get ready
for grade level courses.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.230
They start with third grade,
get ready for third grade

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.930
and go all the way to
get ready for precalculus

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.510
and they're built to help students

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.700
who are going into that upcoming course

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.408
prepare for that, the course
that they're headed into

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:40.800
the math course they're headed into.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.283
- And why did we do this?

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.540
- Yeah, we did this because we know

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.500
every year there's a summer slide, right?

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.400
Students lose a little bit
of what they've learned

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.720
in the last year, over the summer.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.576
But this year with the
school closures due to COVID,

00:02:53.576 --> 00:02:57.060
we knew that was gonna be
even greater than normal

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.290
and we wanted to find a
way to help address that.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.080
And so the courses are
really to help students,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.060
let's say, going into fifth grade

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.620
with here we know this is the content

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.240
you're about to take on in fifth grade

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.090
and the topics you're gonna explore

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.760
and to have a really successful
year with those topics.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.680
Here's some skills that we think

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.570
would really help you
prepare and help you be ready

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.070
to take on those next topics.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.870
So we built a smaller courses
that really get you ready

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.020
for what you're about
to learn in the Fall.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.259
- Yeah, this notion, this is

00:03:27.259 --> 00:03:28.540
this isn't a covert thing.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.998
This is even pre-COVID kids you
know we talk a lot about it.

00:03:31.998 --> 00:03:35.100
As they're moved through
kind of a fixed pay system,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.471
they inevitably accumulate gaps.

00:03:37.471 --> 00:03:39.010
And then you have things
like Summer Vacation

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.170
where not only do you, not,

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.250
not only do you not learn,
but you often forget,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.470
and some of and the skills
that you did know, atrophy,

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.810
and then those gaps that
you have continued to exist,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.970
or maybe even get bigger.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.070
That's always an issue,

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.000
which is why we've always talked about

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.530
the value of personalized learning,

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.860
being able to learn at
your own time and pace.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.210
But this year, to your point,

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.640
it's likely to be that much worse

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.770
because some students have
been able to keep learning

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.080
on say Khan Academy while others haven't.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.330
And so these get ready
for grade level courses

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:11.210
are way for students, parents,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.530
or teachers working with those students

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:17.190
to make sure they have all
of the prerequisite skills

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.420
up to and including the grade level,

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.700
up to the grade level work

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.880
and then they can of course work on

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.100
at their own time in place.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.090
And I mean, this sounds,
we had Tim Vandenberg

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.200
on, I guess we call it the
show, we had him on this show.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.270
It seems like a lifetime ago,

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.789
I think it was about two months ago now.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:36.890
And, you know he did
something similar with kind of

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.360
Khan Academy before the
get ready for grade level.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.210
He had all of his sixth graders,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.600
90% of whom were at a below grade level

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.986
when they entered into his classroom,

00:04:44.986 --> 00:04:47.310
he had them all started early learning,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.510
kindergarten level math,

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.060
and then go through all of
arithmetic on Khan Academy,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.350
which has a lot of skills.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.780
And he's seen really incredible
gains with those students.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.670
They were not only able to
catch up to sixth grade,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:57.720
they were able to learn sixth grade.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.033
And many of them ended
up ahead of grade level.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.684
How is this different than what kid

00:05:02.684 --> 00:05:04.265
than what Tim Vandenberg does

00:05:04.265 --> 00:05:07.370
or has been doing with his students?

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.188
- Yeah, what Tim does is so exciting,

00:05:09.188 --> 00:05:12.250
starting all the way back in
kindergarten and going through,

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.980
what's slightly different
about these courses,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.650
they're more focused
just on the one course

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.850
you're about to go into.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.520
So they're really, we
really thought about,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:23.840
you know, what does a seventh
grade math student take on?

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.124
What are the topics of seventh
grade math student learns

00:05:26.124 --> 00:05:29.840
and built like a really
custom set of content

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.316
and exercises and videos

00:05:31.316 --> 00:05:33.770
that support that very specific grade.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.030
So they fill the gaps just like Tim does,

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.480
but they're filling a very personalized

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.400
or like the individualized set of gaps

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.990
that we know for that course.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.903
So it's sort of like a
fast tracked Tim Model.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.875
- Yeah, so far what's going on here

00:05:50.875 --> 00:05:53.810
is essentially we want all
teachers to be able to do,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:54.790
all students to be able to do

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.356
exactly what the students in
Tim Vandenberg class does.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:05:59.760
But Tim Vandenberg was kind
of using existing things

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.590
on Khan Academy and his students
who maybe have to cover,

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:04.710
you know third grade
or fourth grade skills

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.950
that don't directly feed
into the sixth grade.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.080
And so what we've essentially done

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.480
is taken Tim Vandenberg's intent,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:12.870
but we've streamlined it,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:16.270
so that teachers and students
have a much better chance

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.640
of getting to grade level
as quickly as possible.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.510
And how, how would you
recommend teachers do use it?

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.173
Would you say that they should

00:06:26.173 --> 00:06:28.080
have all their students work on it

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.220
